# CEILING2 — what is the ceiling on a clean path, and which lever raises it: beam width or catalogue support?

Workspace `/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling2-ws`. Interpreter
`/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover/.venv/bin/python` only. **Never modify**
`/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover` or its `.venv`. **Design-phase exploration, not a claim.**

**Resource limits, enforced.** **Hard cap: 3 python processes, `nice -n 15`, all BLAS threads
= 1, peak RSS under 5 GB.** Live trainings and jobs must not be slowed. Print peak RSS and an
`anchor k/N` line.

## Why this is being redone

Every ceiling figure this project has quoted — coordination `+1.944795%`, acceleration
`+1.045609%` / `+1.840863%`, traversal `+0.905261%`, and the whole `BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP`
table — was computed with the certified first-improvement fixed point at **`13.430253`**.

`STATIC-BASELINE-FAMILY-CLEANPATH-2026-09-10.md` (`/home/sat/mcrl-v025-rank2-ws`) verified —
by replacing `StepEvaluator.evaluate` with a fail-closed raising stub and asserting **zero**
scalar calls — that on a clean dense path that fixed point is **`31.028111`**, retaining
**0 of 12** published configuration IDs. It is a different configuration, not a rounding
difference. `MYOPIC_GREEDY` moved `28.668530 -> 31.078504`; the four direct rules did not move
at all.

**A ceiling is a ratio to that fixed point. Every one of them is therefore wrong, and most
likely too large**, because the true fixed point is more than twice as strong as the number
they were divided by.

**And a second, independent limit was found and never followed up.**
`C3-DECLARED-TARGET-LEARNABILITY-2026-09-10.md` (`/home/sat/mcrl-v025-coalgen-ws`) reports the
exact bounded catalogue is **78.72% `|A|=1`**, **19.31% `|A|=2`** — **`|A| <= 2` is 98.13%** of
36,788 rows. `KILL-TRIAGE-2026-09-10.md` gives the searched support as about **142 orders of
magnitude** smaller than the nominal joint action space.

**So "the coordination ceiling" is currently "what moves of at most two users can get", not
"what coordination can get".** That is a property of the candidate set, and the candidate set
is a design choice.

## Part 1 — the clean baseline, at the sealed design point

Recompute, at the sealed one-sided half-power angle **`1.66 deg`**, on the same 12 frozen
development anchors, full-buffer numerator, no demand cap:

- the **coordination** ceiling (bounded joint selector versus the fixed point),
- the **traversal-order** gap (first-improvement versus best-improvement),
- and the **acceleration** ceiling (fixed point versus the configuration actually held at 10 s).

**Parity is against `STATICS2`'s clean values, not the published ones.** Reproduce
`NEAREST_ELIGIBLE = 11.027760`, `RSS_MAX = 41.621560`, `MYOPIC_GREEDY = 31.078504` and
`FIRST_IMPROVEMENT_FP = 31.028111` before reporting anything else. **If you cannot, stop.**

**Report the acceleration ceiling with an explicit warning.** `BEAMSWEEP` measured the same
quantity twice and got `+1.045609%` and `+1.840863%`, differing only because the search
"completed more moves within ten wall-clock seconds" under different machine load. **A ceiling
that moves with machine load is not a stable quantity.** Report the load during your run and
say so in the report.

## Part 2 — the catalogue-support lever, which is the new question

Hold beam width at `1.66 deg`. Vary **only** the coalition-size support of the bounded joint
catalogue and measure how the coordination ceiling responds:

- `|A| <= 1` (unilateral only),
- `|A| <= 2` (the current effective support),
- `|A| <= 3`, `<= 4`, `<= 6`,
- plus complete beam evacuations, which the current catalogue already contains.

For each: the coordination ceiling, the number of catalogue rows, **the number of anchors where
a strict joint improvement occurs**, the mean changed-set size of the winning row, and wall
seconds per anchor.

**The number this part exists for: does the ceiling keep rising with support, or does it
saturate?** If it saturates at `|A| = 2`, the current catalogue is not the binding constraint
and the interaction route has genuinely little to do. **If it keeps rising, the ceiling is a
choice this project has been making without knowing it.** Either answer is decisive; report
whichever you measure.

Cap the work honestly: state the row cap you apply at each support level and whether the
catalogue was exhausted or truncated. **A truncated catalogue understates its own ceiling** —
say so wherever it happens.

## Part 3 — beam width, on the clean path

Repeat Part 1's three ceilings at **`2.40 deg`**, the only sampled width in `BEAMSWEEP` that
combined higher absolute fixed-point and joint EE, higher service (`1063-1075/1200`) and higher
rate-target attainment (`176-180/1200`) with a materially larger acceleration ceiling.

**Report absolute pooled EE alongside every ceiling.** `BEAMSWEEP`'s own caution stands: at
`3.32 deg` the ceiling was largest and the operating point was worst. **A larger ceiling on a
worse base is not progress**, and the report must make that impossible to misread.

Run `3.32 deg` only if Parts 1-3 finish comfortably.

## Part 4 — the comparison the owner needs

One table: **which lever moves the ceiling more per unit of cost** — beam width, or catalogue
coalition support? State the cost of each in wall seconds per anchor and in what it would change
in the system: beam width is a **physical design parameter** and changes the deployed system;
catalogue support is a **decision-time compute** parameter and changes the 10 s budget.

**Say plainly whether either lever gets the coordination ceiling into double digits**, and at
what price. The owner's standing concern is that a 1% ceiling makes the whole exercise
pointless regardless of how well anything is learned.

